---
title: BoosterTutor, Clearing the Log Jam
blurb: The Arena import is live, and the first real draft it reviewed was mine. It did not go well, for me or for the import.
date: 2026-10-26
tags: [web]
---

The first real draft log I fed to the Arena import was refused outright, with every
single card unmatched. Not a great start for a feature I'd spent the better part of a
week on.

The parser was fine. The problem was the card data. Arena identifies cards by its own
internal IDs, and I'd been relying on Scryfall to map those to actual cards, but
Scryfall didn't have them for Reality Fracture yet. 17Lands does publish them in a
public card list, so the data workflow now reads that list, matches each card by name,
and keeps the IDs in a fallback table. If a card is still missing, you get a short
message saying BoosterTutor doesn't know it yet, instead of a pile of errors.

With that fixed, I uploaded the same draft on the live site on October 24 and got a
full review back. The record at the top read 0-3. That one's on me, not the parser.

## What leaves your computer

`Player.log` is the file Arena writes on your machine when detailed logging is on. It
records every pack you see and every card you take, but it also holds your screen
name, your account ID and an auth token, none of which I want anywhere near my
server. So the browser does the filtering before anything is uploaded. A background
worker streams through the file and only stops on lines that begin with a few known
markers. From each of those it copies a fixed set of fields into a fresh object, never
the raw line. You then get a list of the drafts it found, and before you send one you
can see exactly what's in the payload. The actual parsing still happens in Python,
since the rule from day one is that Python works out every fact.

Real logs turned out to be messier than anything I'd read about them. One of mine had
a draft split in two by a game restart, with a dropped connection somewhere in the
middle, so a handful of picks simply aren't in the file. I went back and forth on
refusing drafts like that and ended up reviewing them anyway. The parser fills in what
it can from your final pool, and the review works with what's there. It also reads
your match record now, which is how mine knew about the 0-3.

## Practice drafts, no account needed

You can now practice draft without signing in. The browser holds a random secret for
your draft and the server keeps only a hash of it, so the draft is yours without an
account attached. Drafts also start out unsaved. Hit Save Draft and it becomes a Study,
and if you were signed out, signing in first claims it. Anything left unsaved for a
day gets cleaned up when the next draft starts.

While I was in there, I noticed FRA's pack library was eating about 35 MB of memory on
a server that only has 512. It's around 170,000 opening packs, stored as nested
tuples. Packing every card into two bytes in one flat array brought it down to
4.6 MB.

## The boring, important stuff

Most of the last few days went into Phase 9, which is hardening. None of it shows up
on the page, but it's what lets me sleep once strangers are using this. Every request
now carries an ID that appears in the logs and in any error message, so a bug report
can point me at the exact request. Errors from the API and the website both go to
Sentry, CI scans for leaked secrets, and Dependabot keeps an eye on packages. An
accessibility pass also turned up a bunch of keyboard and screen reader problems that
are now fixed.

My favorite fix came out of an actual incident. A few times Render just didn't deploy
after a merge, and the database migrations sat unapplied until I noticed and deployed
by hand. Now the health check compares the database's migration version against the
one the code expects, and reports unhealthy when they differ. There's also a daily job
that totals up yesterday's Claude spending and emails me if it passes $3, so I hear
about it well before the $5 daily cap does anything. Somewhere in the middle of all
this I upgraded to SvelteKit 3, and it promptly broke half of the Sentry setup.

A couple of launch items also jumped the queue. Gnarl's Notes now end with "Was this
helpful?" and a thumbs up or down, with room for a comment if you want to leave one.
Each rating is stored with the prompt version that wrote the note, so once people are
actually using it I'll know which prompt coaches better. The footer picked up the Fan
Content Policy notice that Wizards requires, a short Terms page, and the Ko-fi link I
mentioned last time.

There's a little of Phase 9 left, and the help page still needs a log from someone on
a Mac. Then I'll start letting other people in, and hopefully their records look
better than mine.
